      "category": "article",
      "title": "Ridley Scott’s ‘Napoleon’ Complex",
      "creator": "Michael Schulman for The New Yorker_",
      "note": "for _The New Yorker_. [Ridley Scott](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridley_Scott) is the filmmaker behind cult classics and mega-blockbusters, such as _Blade Runner_, _Gladiator_, _Alien, Black Hawk Down_, and many more. He is also the director behind the [iconic Apple “1984” commercial](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ErwS24cBZPc). Millions know his movies, but far fewer realize that Ridley didn’t release his first feature film until he was 40. Bonus: for a 10-second laugh involving Ridley Scott that you won’t get in the profile, [take a gander at this](https://www.instagram.com/p/CzUkLMrvZxf/?igshid=YjVjNjZkNmFjNg%3D%3D).",
